# Routh I.Arts.407-408 + 412 — Lagrange's equilateral three-body solution, and
# why it does not survive — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "A Treatise on Dynamics of a Particle" (Cambridge, 1898),
# Part I, Art. 407: "Let the several particles be so arranged in a plane that
# the resultant accelerating force on each passes through the common centre of
# gravity O of the system and that each resultant is proportional to the
# distance of the particle from that centre."
#
# Art. 408 shows there are exactly TWO such arrangements of three particles --
# the collinear one and the EQUILATERAL one. In the equilateral case the three
# bodies sit at the corners of an equilateral triangle and the whole figure
# turns rigidly about its centroid, keeping its shape forever.
#
# THE DERIVATION. Put three equal masses m at the corners of an equilateral
# triangle of side s. Each feels two attractions of magnitude G*m^2/s^2, at 60
# degrees to one another, whose resultant points at the centroid with magnitude
#
#     2 * (G m^2 / s^2) * cos(30) = sqrt(3) * G m^2 / s^2
#
# The distance from a corner to the centroid is R = s/sqrt(3), so the
# centripetal requirement m*omega^2*R gives
#
#     sqrt(3) G m^2 / s^2 = m omega^2 s / sqrt(3)     =>     omega^2 = 3 G m / s^3
#
# THE NUMBERS. G = 1, m = 1, s = 1:
#
#     omega  = sqrt(3) = 1.7320508075688772
#     R      = 1/sqrt(3) = 0.5773502691896258
#     speed  = omega*R = 1 exactly
#     period = 2*pi/sqrt(3) = 3.6275987284684357
#
# The cells run one full period and print each body's distance from where it
# started: all three return to ~1e-13, and the triangle side is still 1.
#
# ART. 412 -- IT IS UNSTABLE. Routh proves the corresponding collinear motion
# unstable "if the force vary as any inverse power of the distance", and the
# equilateral case has its own criterion, which for equal masses reads
#
#     (m1 m2 + m2 m3 + m3 m1) / (m1 + m2 + m3)^2  =  3/9 = 1/3
#
# against the threshold 1/27 = 0.037. One third is far above it, so three EQUAL
# masses cannot hold the formation. The second half of this notebook displaces
# one body by a thousandth and runs on: the triangle, which was exact to 1e-13,
# comes apart.
#
# SETUP NOTES. G = 1 and softening = 0 for an exact 1/r^2 law. The bodies are
# points, so nothing collides; in the perturbed run they may pass close, which
# is the instability doing its work rather than a numerical fault.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: first a triangle turning rigidly, as though welded. Then the
# same triangle with one corner nudged a thousandth of a unit -- which holds
# its shape for a while, then visibly distorts and breaks up.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/youruser/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p1_equilateral_three_body
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_equilateral_three_body.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_equilateral_three_body.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 1
set system.softening = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15

new point { mass = 1, position = [ 0.5773502691896258,  0,                   0], velocity = [ 0,                   1,                    0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [-0.2886751345948128,  0.5000000000000001,  0], velocity = [-0.8660254037844387, -0.49999999999999983, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [-0.2886751345948132, -0.4999999999999999,  0], velocity = [ 0.8660254037844384, -0.5000000000000004,  0] }

# side length 1, speed 1, centre of mass at the origin
norm(obj0.position - obj1.position)
norm(obj0.velocity)
momentum

run 3.6275987284684357 steps 6

# one full period: every body home, and the triangle still exactly a triangle
norm(obj0.position - [ 0.5773502691896258, 0, 0])
norm(obj1.position - [-0.2886751345948128,  0.5000000000000001, 0])
norm(obj2.position - [-0.2886751345948132, -0.4999999999999999, 0])
norm(obj0.position - obj1.position)
reset

# ---- Art. 412: nudge one corner by 0.001 and the formation comes apart
set system.g_constant = 1
set system.softening = 1e-6
set system.rtol = 1e-12
set system.atol = 1e-14
new point { mass = 1, position = [ 0.5783502691896258,  0,                   0], velocity = [ 0,                   1,                    0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [-0.2886751345948128,  0.5000000000000001,  0], velocity = [-0.8660254037844387, -0.49999999999999983, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [-0.2886751345948132, -0.4999999999999999,  0], velocity = [ 0.8660254037844384, -0.5000000000000004,  0] }

# the three side lengths, initially 1.0005, 1, 1.0005
norm(obj0.position - obj1.position)
norm(obj1.position - obj2.position)
run 10.882796185405307 steps 9
# after three periods the sides no longer agree
norm(obj0.position - obj1.position)
norm(obj1.position - obj2.position)
norm(obj2.position - obj0.position)
run 21.765592370810614 steps 12
# after nine, the triangle is gone
norm(obj0.position - obj1.position)
norm(obj1.position - obj2.position)
norm(obj2.position - obj0.position)
reset

set system.g_constant = 1
set system.softening = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new point { mass = 1, position = [ 0.5773502691896258,  0,                   0], velocity = [ 0,                   1,                    0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [-0.2886751345948128,  0.5000000000000001,  0], velocity = [-0.8660254037844387, -0.49999999999999983, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [-0.2886751345948132, -0.4999999999999999,  0], velocity = [ 0.8660254037844384, -0.5000000000000004,  0] }
energy

scene create
scene zoom in
scene set_time_step 0.02
